ar the Tate and Lambeth Bridge. Treat \"where the embankment gave way\" with care: there was overtopping at many points along both banks, and a single named breach point is often journalistic shorthand for a more distributed failure. Your piece will be stronger, and safer, if it distinguishes the breach from the overtopping.\n\n**Low confidence — do not print without a source**\n- The geographic split of the 14 deaths. I recall something like ten in the Westminster/Pimlico area (Grosvenor Road) and four in Hammersmith, including a group of young women in one basement room. I am not confident in that division, and it is exactly the sort of detail that gets garbled between retellings.\n- **Peak water level.** I'm deliberately not giving you a figure. I recall it was a record at the time, but I don't trust my recall of the number or its datum (above Ordnance Datum vs. chart datum vs. Trinity High Water — these differ by metres and are routinely confused).\n\n## Verification worklist\n\nThese are **leads, not citations** — don't print any of them until you've had the document in front of you.\n\n1. **The Times, 7 and 9 January 1928.** Contemporary reporting, including casualty lists and the breach location. Times Digital Archive, usually free via a UK library card.\n2. **Hansard**, Commons debates January–February 1928 on Thames flooding and embankment repair. Free and full-text searchable at hansard.parliament.uk — the fastest citable source for official death toll and remedial works.\n3. **Tate's own published history** on the 1928 flood and the Turner damage — tate.org.uk. Best source for the gallery material, and citable as an institutional author.\n4. **Port of London Authority** tide records, for the peak level with its datum stated explicitly.\n5. **Environment Agency / Thames Barrier** historical background, for the 1928 → 1953 → Barrier causal chain. Note that 1953's North Sea flood, not 1928, was the decisive driver for the Barrier; if your piece implies otherwise, that's a substantive error.\n6. **London Metropolitan Archives** for LCC records on the embankment repairs.\n\nTwo of these — Hansard and Tate — are free, fast, and cover your two headline asks. That's plausibly a 30-minute job tonight.\n\nIf you re-run this with web access granted, I'll verify each fact, pull the real citations with author/title/publication/year, and mark anything that stays contested. I'd also flag that \"the 1928 Thames flood\" is sometimes confused in secondary sources with the 1953 East Coast flood — worth a deliberate check that any source you lean on isn't conflating them.\n\nWant me to build this out as a publishable page once the facts are verified?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":354,"cache_read_input_tokens":32287,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":354},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b11c0451-d2f9-4b41-84cd-701e9532f99e","uuid":"9282c5dc-1a42-4a6f-ae72-5434b25d8bf7","timestamp":"2026-09-25T07:09:12.972Z","request_id":"req_011CfPjhJmYi6bEoPPT43jnL"}
